Z||000010||Smoooooth GT||10-09-2001||12:18 AM||||wow...  it happened to me also...  I got slammed in the passenger side door..  he knocked me into oncoming traffic and got it head in then....  poor 87 GT...  <BR>I got another and got the idea for the Smooooth GT...  Don't give up on these car's.. they are GREAT...    Steve<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/totalled_gt_inside_3.jpg"> <BR>This is a INSIDE SHOT of the passebger side door and dash....  pretty tough car...  It was like a NASCAR wreck..  I had no control after the first impact...   <P>------------------<BR>NOS Injected 2.8L V/6<BR>UNDER CONSTRUCTION   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooth_sig2_4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||10-09-2001||12:24 AM||||As soon as a person is unable to do a panic stop, or successfully swerve out of the way to avoid a collision, they should stop driving.. I used to know an old guy, in his 90s and still driving.. he could barely see over the steering wheel, took him 5 mins to get into his car and back out of the driveway cause he moved so slow. now what are the chances he'd be able to react fast enough if a kid ran out in front of him, or another car, etc...<P>I've almost been in several accidents because elderly drivers weren't driving properly.. one of my biggest pet peeves is old people driving huge boats, and not looking where they're going, driving thru stopsigns, pulling out in front of you, etc..<BR>however, there are bad drivers in every age group.. last thursday on the way home from work I was passing a dump truck that was going 70kph on the highway.. as soon as I passed, he swerved into my lane for no apparent reason, and I was forced off the road. luckily the gravel shoulder was fairly wide, and I was able to keep from going into the ditch. I have no idea why the driver did that, and I didn't hang around to find out.. I got back on the road in my own lane, and kept the pedal floored until I was at least half a km ahead....<P>but overall, old drivers are by far worse in my books than younger drivers.. I'm certainly not a model driver, (as anyone who knows me will attest.....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> ) but myself and other younger drivers have the ability to react fast to a situation, old people often don't.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||4
Z||000012||BN Boomer||10-09-2001||10:40 AM||||Before everyone jumps on the Bash the Old people bandwagon, how about some stats showing which age group is responsible for more accidents. My bet is that young men cause much more mayhem than old people. Better reflexes or not, young, aggresive drivers are a far bigger threat than some old geezer plodding along. Insurance rates probably reflect that, too.<P>And let's wait until you guys are that old and see how easily you surrender your own drivers license along with the mobility and independence that being able to drive provides.<P>I agree that it should be much more difficult to get a driver's license in the first place and then keep it through the years. I like the graduated licensing system Washington just adopted that limits when young, beginning drives are able to drive and who they can drive with. Everyone else needs to be checked every few years for competency, too.<P>I rather be hit by some old person moving slowly than by some punk out street racing.||||reg||1
Z||000013||x-thumpr-x||10-09-2001||11:08 AM||||Well, around here in Toronto, you got to watch out for the chineses drivers and pakistan cabbies. Have avoid several accidents from orientals not checking there blind spots, and cabbies will be cabbies! I installed a fiam air horn on the car, so if they don't see me, they'll hear me!<P>------------------<BR> Where there's a Willie, there's a way!<P>Willie..||||reg||1
Z||000014||Voytek||10-09-2001||11:39 AM||||I think I've seen just as many old people drive stupid as I've seen young people.<P>There is a real push out there to do something about the young drivers - British Columbia, for example, has adopted a new system in which a young driver goes through stages (it includes day-light driving limits, stickers on cars, etc.).<P>There isn't, however, anyone pushing to test old people.  All too often I see an old person (man OR woman) changing lanes without even so much as checking the mirror.  I've seen them do a lot worse than that, too.  The fact is that the older you get, the less capable you are - no question about it.<P>Why do we not restrict them?  Here's my opinion: the older folks vote while the 16 year olds can't.  Just try to impose restrictions on a few million seniors and see if you win the next elections.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||stimpy||10-09-2001||11:55 AM||||Boomer, where the hell have you been? I was starting to think they put you in a nursing home without internet access, ya old geez! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Seriously though, I think the relatives of these aincient drivers need to have the stones to take away mom or dads keys when they get to old to drive. A little personal responsibility when it comes to making tough choices goes a long way to keeping society safe. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||stsmithgt||10-09-2001||03:42 PM||||I know right where your coming from when it comes to getting pushed around with no control of the cituation.<BR>More tests would be fine.  Maybe even skill level sticker to warn you about the bad drivers like rookie cars get tagged on the track, but it wouldn't screen out the 27 year old guy who pushed my car around with a Caddy.  He's the type that's easily distracted and didn't get his attention back on the road before he put his foot into the gas peddle.  Passing a drivers test would probably be easy for him.<P>There was a bunch of those unbelievably BAD drivers out there the last few days.  It's like they can't think and drive at the same time. <I> Anyway..... . .  .   .    .</I>||||reg||1
Z||000017||x-thumpr-x||10-09-2001||04:52 PM||||Here in Ontario, we have graduated liscence. There is three stages:<P>First is G1:  where the card holder can only drive during daylight hours with someone who has 4 years experience an not on any 400 series highway.<P>Second is G2: is the same as G1 but the card holder is allowed to drive alone.<P>Last is G3(full Liscence): Do whatever you want as long as you don't get caught.||||reg||1
